    <h1>So… why “Cabbage”?</h1>
    <p class="cc26-morph" aria-label="Gryckiewicz, sounds like Gry-kevich, sounds like Gray Cabbage, became Cabbage Coins">
        <span>Gryckiewicz</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>“Gry-kevich”</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>“Gray <b>Cabbage</b>”</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span class="cc26-morph__end"><b>Cabbage</b> Coins</span>
    </p>
</section>

<article class="cc26-wrap cc26-storybody">
    <div class="cc26-storytext">
        <p class="cc26-storytext__lead">It's one of the questions we're asked most often, and the answer is a lot less fancy than you'd think. It starts with a last name that nobody can pronounce.</p>
        <p>Say <em>Gryckiewicz</em> out loud and it comes out something like “Gry-kevich” — which sounds a whole lot like <strong>“Gray Cabbage.”</strong> That became the nickname, and when Tony started poking around Instagram for numismatic posts and pages dedicated to rare coins, his account was <strong>@tonygraycabbage</strong>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cc26-storytext">
        <p>When Tony found his first coins to try selling, he sold them right from that @tonygraycabbage profile. As he sold more, he made a dedicated page and called it <strong>Cabbage Coins</strong>.</p>
    </div>

