    {# Two fun facts #}
    <div class="cc26-funfacts">
        <aside class="cc26-funfact">
            <span class="cc26-funfact__tag">Fun fact</span>
            <h2>“Cabbage” means money</h2>
            <p>Turns out the name works on two levels. <strong>“Cabbage” is slang for money</strong> — paper money especially, after the green of the leaves. It's American in origin, on record since the early 1900s, and later picked up in Britain too. It's part of the same leafy family as <em>lettuce</em> and <em>kale</em>, and in South Africa the green ten-rand note was nicknamed a “cabbage.”</p>
            <p class="cc26-funfact__source">Source: Green's Dictionary of Slang</p>
        </aside>
        <aside class="cc26-funfact cc26-funfact--coin">
            <span class="cc26-funfact__tag">Tony's favorite coin</span>
            <h2>Latvia's 2024 “Cabbage”</h2>
            <div class="cc26-funfact__pair">
                <img src="/assets/img/story-latvia-reverse.jpg" alt="Tony's 2024 Latvia Kāposts 5 euro coin: overlapping cabbage leaves with LATVIJA and 5 EURO" loading="lazy">
                <img src="/assets/img/story-latvia-obverse.jpg" alt="The other side of Tony's Kāposts coin: a cabbage cut through, with 2024 at its core" loading="lazy">
            </div>
            <p>In 2024 the Bank of Latvia issued <strong>“Kāposts” (Cabbage)</strong>, a €5 silver proof shaped like a head of cabbage — seen from above on one side and cut through on the other, with “2024” at its core. The bank picked the cabbage for its layers of meaning, wealth included: green leaves like banknotes, and “cabbage” as slang for money.</p>
            <p>It's Tony's favorite coin and the only thing he religiously collects today. It's in good company, too — it was named <strong>Best Silver Coin at the 2025 Coin of the Year awards</strong>.</p>
            <p class="cc26-funfact__source">€5 · .999 silver · 23 g · mintage 3,500 · designed by Mārtiņš Rozenfelds · struck at the Lithuanian Mint</p>
        </aside>
    </div>
